{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## /rebar-debug investigation — Part 2 (precedent, OSS norms, RECOMMENDATION)\n\n### Q4 — Precedent: reuse vs. add-vocabulary\n- **dbca-97ac-ad96-4d6d (potential-powellite-mouflon, merged `ae4f6c1a`, Gerrit 2350) — ADDED vocabulary (`not_found`, `invalid_input`).** Justification bar it cleared: a **real, observed misclassification on a LIVE surface** — `render_workflow`/`get_workflow_status` (documented always-available MCP READ tools) returned `llm_unavailable` for caller typos, defeating the machine-readable failure contract in `docs/mcp-reference.md`. Growing the taxonomy was warranted because a live agent branching on the code was actively misled. It also updated the full contract surface (`schemas/common.schema.json`, generated `docs/mcp-reference.md` via `gen_mcp_reference.py`, `api_surface_baseline.json`) + held-out oracle + mutation check. Closed `plan_defect`, caused_by `8a31`.\n- **35ab-f0e0-3e4a-4b72 (this ticket's parent, merged `f854924b`, Gerrit 2395) — REUSED existing `invalid_input`, added NO vocabulary.** The executor unknown-`uses:`-step site was **latent** (interpreter catches the raise into a failed `StepResult` string; a runtime oracle proved `error_code_for` is never invoked). They fixed it anyway as **defense-in-depth**, but only because an unknown-step workflow is *semantically a clean fit* for the existing `invalid_input` (\"found-but-invalid workflow\", same class as `WorkflowValidationError`). And they **explicitly SPLIT OUT this editor site** (per the /rebar-debug escalate-design-decisions guard) precisely because (a) it needs a NEW-vocabulary design call and (b) it is not observably misclassified.\n\n**Standing project intent (from this history + docs):** grow the error-code taxonomy only when a **live consumer** observes the wrong code against a **documented contract** (dbca); for latent/defense-in-depth gaps, **reuse an existing code IF one fits semantically** (35ab). No ADR mandates growth; the contract lives in `docs/mcp-reference.md` \"Failure shape\" + `KNOWN_ERROR_CODES` + `common.schema.json`.\n\n### Q5 — OSS norms for \"missing/not-found artifact\" vs \"invalid input\" vs \"unavailable\"\nA dedicated not-found class IS the norm — BUT mature taxonomies further separate a *caller-named* not-found from a *system-state/precondition* failure, which is exactly the distinction that matters here:\n- **gRPC:** `NOT_FOUND(5)` (entity the caller referenced) is distinct from `INVALID_ARGUMENT(3)`, from `UNAVAILABLE(14)` (retryable outage), AND from **`FAILED_PRECONDITION(9)`** — \"system is not in a state required for the operation\" (the canonical bucket for \"environment not built/ready\"). A missing local build artifact is a textbook `FAILED_PRECONDITION`, NOT `NOT_FOUND`.\n- **HTTP:** `404` vs `400` vs `503` — not-found is first-class; a missing server-side dependency is typically `503`/`500`, not `404`.\n- **POSIX/errno, Python, Rust:** `ENOENT`/`FileNotFoundError`/`io::ErrorKind::NotFound` are distinct from `EINVAL`/`ValueError`/`InvalidInput`. Rust even adds `Unsupported`.\nTakeaway: folding a missing *server-side build artifact* into caller `not_found` or `invalid_input` is against the grain of these norms; the honest analog is a precondition/environment class (or a generic operational failure).\n\n### RECOMMENDATION → **C (defer)**, with a specified A-path for later\n**Defer the taxonomy change now.** Rationale:\n1. **No observable defect.** Site is latent + CLI-only; zero live consumers receive any code. dbca's bar for growing vocabulary (a live, contract-backed misclassification) is **not met**; 35ab's basis for a defense-in-depth reuse (a *clean semantic fit* with an existing code) is **also not met**.\n2. **No existing code fits (rules out B).** `not_found` is contractually \"caller named an unknown workflow/run\" — using it here would blur the very fix-your-input-vs-outage line dbca drew (a caller would read `not_found` as \"I mistyped\"). `invalid_input` is wrong (caller input is valid). `command_failed` is merely the generic fallback. So B buys mis-precision, not precision.\n3. **A is premature contract growth.** A new code is a *published* contract every consumer must henceforth understand; minting one for an unreachable path pollutes `KNOWN_ERROR_CODES`/`common.schema.json`/generated docs/baseline for no live benefit.\n4. The CLI already emits a clear, actionable remedy (\"Build it with: npm ... build\"), so user experience today is fine.\n\n**Deferred A-path (adopt IF/WHEN `edit_workflow` is ever exposed via MCP or otherwise routed through `error_code_for`):** add ONE new code for the environment/precondition class — proposed **`environment_unready`** (alt names: `asset_unavailable`, `dependency_unbuilt`), semantics: *\"a required local build artifact or environment dependency is absent; the operation cannot run until the environment is built — not caller input, not an LLM/provider outage.\"* Implement mirroring 35ab: a dedicated `WorkflowError` subclass (e.g. `WorkflowAssetsUnavailableError`) + a `_workflow_caller_code`/`error_code_for` mapping, keep bare `WorkflowError`→`llm_unavailable` for execute-time outages, update `common.schema.json` + generated `docs/mcp-reference.md` + `api_surface_baseline.json`, held-out oracle asserting the CODE, mutation-checked. Consumers needing to honor it = **only the new MCP surface** that exposes edit (today: zero). Explicitly do NOT overload `not_found`.\n\n### caused_by candidate + likely class (if a fix were ever done)\n- **caused_by:** `8a31-e1c3-cf31-4239` (biomedical-upper-tench, commit `77f1c4011c83`) — the blanket `LLMError`→`llm_unavailable` mapping; same origin dbca/35ab both cite.\n- **likely close class:** `plan_defect` (consistent with dbca and 35ab), were the deferred A-path implemented.\n\nLeaving ticket `in_progress` for the operator's decision (not closing).\n"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788055357904621422,"uuid":"7c1b133f-5e74-4e7c-bdc5-569d9a8a52d6"}